const passages={
  1:{title:'The Measure of Prosperity',by:'Clara Avery — 1908',prompt:'After reading Clara Avery’s speech “The Measure of Prosperity,” write an introductory paragraph for an essay that analyzes the rhetorical choices Avery makes to convey her message to her audience.',html:`
<p class="headnote"><em>In 1908, during a period of rapid industrial expansion, civic writer Clara Avery delivered the following address.</em></p>
<p><b>[1]</b> We are accustomed to congratulating ourselves upon every enlargement of industry, as though abundance, merely because it has multiplied, must therefore have been wisely distributed. We point with pride to the new mills along the canal, to the freight yards that no longer sleep, to the warehouses whose windows burn like small suns. These are achievements, and I would be the last to deny them.</p>
<p><b>[2]</b> But prosperity becomes a narrow word when it counts what a city produces and forgets the conditions under which its people produce it. We count the tons of steel. We count the cars loaded before dawn. We count the value of land that yesterday seemed worthless. Yet a ledger that records every shipment may still omit the fatigue of the hand that packed it, the rent demanded of the family beside the factory, and the uncertainty of the worker who knows that one week of illness may consume a month of wages.</p>
<p><b>[3]</b> I do not ask that we distrust wealth, nor that we romanticize hardship. Poverty possesses no hidden virtue, and idleness becomes no nobler because labor can be abused. I ask only that we apply to prosperity the same intelligence we apply to production: that we inquire not merely how much has been made, but what manner of life has been made possible.</p>
<p><b>[4]</b> Honor the worker who tends the furnace, carrying its heat home in his clothes and its dust in the lines of his hands, who lays the rail and loads the ship and oils the press and returns before sunrise to begin again. The city praises the finished bridge; it should remember the bodies bent above each rivet.</p>
<p><b>[5]</b> A community may grow richer in goods and poorer in security. It may enlarge its factories while diminishing the confidence with which ordinary families face tomorrow. If prosperity is to be more than a splendid display in a merchant’s window, it must enter the home as well as the marketplace.</p>
<p><b>[6]</b> Let us therefore measure success with a fuller instrument. Let us welcome enterprise, but not worship it; reward invention, but not excuse neglect; admire accumulation, but ask what it has accumulated for the common life. The wealth of a city is not proved merely by what rises above its streets. It is proved also by whether those who build the city can remain standing within it.</p>`},
  2:{title:'What Responsibility Requires',by:'Elias Ward — 1911',html:`
<p class="headnote"><em>Three days after a deadly fire at the Mercer Works factory, community leader Elias Ward delivered the following remarks at a public memorial.</em></p>
<p><b>[1]</b> Three days have passed since the bell above the Mercer Works rang before dawn, and still the sound seems to hang over this town. We have seen blackened windows, a scorched stairwell, coats left upon hooks by men who expected to return. Eleven families have learned how suddenly an ordinary morning can divide life into before and after. We gather because grief demands witness; but if grief is to possess moral use, it must demand more.</p>
<p><b>[2]</b> There is a dangerous comfort in the word accident. It allows us to mourn without asking whether warning preceded disaster. We may say the fire was unforeseen. We may say no one intended harm. We may say that industry always carries risk. Each statement may contain some truth, and together they may still conceal the larger one: that what no person intended may nevertheless become what many persons permitted.</p>
<p><b>[3]</b> “Am I my brother’s keeper?” is an old question, but age has not made it obsolete. If a door required repair and remained locked, if an alarm failed and was not replaced, if a complaint was recorded and then buried beneath another day’s business, then before we give the dead to fate, we must ask whether fate has been asked to carry a burden that belongs to human judgment. Can we honor those who died if we refuse to examine what the living might have prevented?</p>
<p><b>[4]</b> Let no one mistake examination for vengeance. The object is not to discover one convenient offender upon whom every failure may be placed. Responsibility is often less dramatic and therefore more difficult: a warning postponed, an inspection abbreviated, a custom tolerated because yesterday it caused no injury. Negligence seldom announces itself as negligence. It acquires authority by repetition.</p>
<p><b>[5]</b> Therefore let us answer sorrow with discipline: to inspect before danger is familiar, to report before silence becomes consent, to repair before economy becomes excuse, and to stop work before risk becomes catastrophe. Let us choose not punishment after calamity, but responsibility before it. The factory may reopen; labor must continue. Yet if the machines resume before conscience resumes its duty, we shall have restored production without restoring trust.</p>
<p><b>[6]</b> The measure of this town will not be how quickly smoke disappears. It will be whether every person who enters that gate can believe convenience will not again be purchased with another person’s safety.</p>`}
};
